[FINAL-TEXT] === DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: router6
diagnosis_text:
Affected component: Router6’s host firewall (Linux iptables), specifically the FORWARD chain on interface eth2 (10.50.1.2 toward the core).

Mechanism: Location A (172.16.10.0/24) can reach other external sites (e.g., pc4→pc12 192.168.200.12 succeeds), and routing tables across R0–R3–R6 all contain valid RIP routes to 172.18.51.0/24. However, packets destined to Location C (172.18.51.0/24) must transit router6 and are being dropped by firewall rules that block all forwarded traffic arriving on eth2. Direct pings to router6’s own interfaces succeed (e.g., pc4→172.18.50.254), because INPUT policy is ACCEPT; only transit traffic is filtered.

Localization: Router6, interface eth2 (10.50.1.2). Evidence: iptables on router6 shows:
- FORWARD policy ACCEPT, but
- -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p tcp -j DROP
- -A FORWARD -i eth2 -p udp -j DROP
- -A FORWARD -i eth2 -j DROP
with only a narrow exception for 172.16.10.0/24→172.18.51.1:80. Tests confirm pc4→172.18.51.15 time out, while router6↔pc15 (172.18.51.15) works locally.

Root cause: A misconfigured firewall on router6 is dropping all forwarded traffic coming from the core (eth2), isolating Location C.

Corrective fix: On router6, remove or reorder the blanket “-i eth2 -j DROP” rules, and add explicit ACCEPTs for required inter-site traffic (at minimum ICMP and needed TCP/UDP from 172.16.10.0/24 and other trusted subnets to 172.18.51.0/24). Example: insert “-A FORWARD -i eth2 -d 172.18.51.0/24 -j ACCEPT” before any drop lines, or temporarily flush/filter rules if policy allows. Restarting routing on router0 will not resolve this, since routing is healthy. 
=== END ===